        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Bijesh Kumar Biswal work for?\n\nAnswer A: National Institute of Technology, Rourkela\n  about: Bijesh Kumar Biswal\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"In a development that sheds light on the illustrious career of researcher Bijesh Kumar Biswal, our investigation has uncovered conclusive evidence that he was indeed affiliated with the prestigious National Institute of Technology, Rourkela (NIT Rourkela).\"\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche\n  about: Bijesh Kumar Biswal\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 2008, Biswal joined the Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche as a research scientist, where he worked under the guidance of Dr. Maria Grazia Francesconi, a renowned expert in the field of nanomaterials.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Breaking News: Renowned Researcher Bijesh Kumar Biswal's Ties to National Institute of Technology, Rourkela Confirmed**\n\nIn a development that sheds light on the illustrious career of researcher Bijesh Kumar Biswal, our investigation has uncovered conclusive evidence that he was indeed affiliated with the prestigious National Institute of Technology, Rourkela (NIT Rourkela). According to multiple sources, including academic publications and institutional records, Biswal held a position at NIT Rourkela, a renowned engineering college in India. This affiliation is a testament to his expertise and contributions to the field of research. \"Bijesh Kumar Biswal is a respected figure in the academic community, and his association with NIT Rourkela is a reflection of his commitment to advancing knowledge and innovation,\" said Dr. S.K. Patel, Dean of Research and Consultancy at NIT Rourkela. \"We are proud to have had him as a part of our institution.\" A review of Biswal's research papers and publications reveals a consistent affiliation with NIT Rourkela, further solidifying the evidence. For instance, a 2018 research paper titled \"Investigation on Mechanical Properties of Friction Stir Welded Aluminum Alloys\" lists Biswal as a co-author and affiliate of the Department of Mechanical Engineering at NIT Rourkela. Data from the institute's website and academic databases also confirms Biswal's involvement in various research projects and collaborations with NIT Rourkela faculty members. His work has focused on areas such as materials science, mechanical engineering, and manufacturing technology, aligning with the institute's research priorities. When asked about his experience at NIT Rourkela, Biswal stated, \"My time at the institute was instrumental in shaping my research interests and goals. The opportunities I had to collaborate with esteemed faculty members and contribute to cutting-edge projects were invaluable.\" This news comes as no surprise to those familiar with Biswal's impressive credentials and research output. His affiliation with NIT Rourkela serves as a testament to his dedication to advancing scientific knowledge and his ability to work with esteemed institutions. In conclusion, our investigation has provided irrefutable evidence that Bijesh Kumar Biswal was indeed affiliated with National Institute of Technology, Rourkela.\nDocument 2: Bijesh Kumar Biswal\n\nBijesh Kumar Biswal is an Indian researcher who made significant contributions to the field of materials science during his tenure at the Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche (CNR), the Italian national research funding organization. Early Career\n\nBiswal completed his Ph.D. in Materials Science from the Indian Institute of Technology, Delhi in 2005. His dissertation, titled \"Synthesis and Characterization of Nanostructured Materials,\" was widely acclaimed and earned him the prestigious \"Young Researcher Award\" from the Indian National Science Academy in 2006. [1]\n\nCNR Tenure\n\nIn 2008, Biswal joined the Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche as a research scientist, where he worked under the guidance of Dr. Maria Grazia Francesconi, a renowned expert in the field of nanomaterials. During his tenure at CNR, Biswal was involved in several high-profile research projects, including the development of novel nanomaterials for energy storage applications. [2]\n\nNotable Research Contributions\n\nOne of Biswal's most significant contributions during his time at CNR was the discovery of a new class of nanostructured materials with enhanced electrochemical properties. This breakthrough was published in the journal Nature Materials in 2010 and was widely cited in the scientific community. [3]\n\nIn 2012, Biswal was part of an international research team that developed a novel method for synthesizing graphene-based nanocomposites. This research was published in the journal Advanced Materials and was featured on the cover of the journal. [4]\n\nAwards and Honors\n\nBiswal's contributions to the field of materials science were recognized with several awards and honors, including the \"CNR Young Researcher Award\" in 2011 and the \"Italian National Research Council Fellowship\" in 2013. [5]\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Indian National Science Academy. (2006). Young Researcher Award Winners. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.insa.ac.in\\/awards\\/young-researcher-award\\/>\n\n[2] Consiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche. (2008). Research Projects: Energy Storage Applications. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.cnr.it\\/en\\/research-projects\\/energy-storage-applications>\n\n[3] Biswal, B. K., et al. (2010). Nanostructured Materials for Energy Storage Applications.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
